==Old English==

===Etymology===
{{proto|gem-pro|fimf|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|ine-pro|lang=ang|pénkʷe|five}}. Cognates include Old High German ''fimf'', ''finf'' (German ''[[fünf]]''), Old Saxon ''fīf'' (Dutch [[vijf]]), Old Norse ''[[fimm]]'' (Swedish ''[[fem]]''), Gothic {{term|𐍆𐌹𐌼𐍆|sc=Goth}}.  The Indo-European root is also the source of Greek {{term|πέντε|tr=pente|lang=el}}, Latin ''[[quinque]]'', Sanskrit {{term|पञ्चन्|lang=sa}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/fiːf/|lang=ang}}

===Numeral===
